ledger-invariants: CANNOT-CATCH — the honest limits of this guard, printed on every run, pass or fail:
ledger-invariants:   1. DYNAMIC SQL. A statement assembled at run time (fmt.Sprintf, strings.Builder, a query
ledger-invariants:      builder's own AST) is not readable as text. The guard converts that blind spot into a
ledger-invariants:      REFUSAL for the mutating driver family (class OPAQUE-SQL) — but only where it recognises
ledger-invariants:      the call. A mutating call through an interface method it does not name, or through
ledger-invariants:      reflection, is invisible.
ledger-invariants:   2. INDIRECTION THROUGH A NAME THAT IS NOT A BALANCE. A field called Amount, Total, Net or
ledger-invariants:      Position that IS a stored balance is not detected: the surface is the NAME, because
ledger-invariants:      without a type checker there is nothing else to key on. Renaming a balance defeats it.
ledger-invariants:   3. STORED PROCEDURES, TRIGGERS AND MIGRATIONS. This guard walks Go only. An UPDATE inside a
ledger-invariants:      Liquibase changelog, a Postgres trigger or a hand-run psql script is out of its reach,
ledger-invariants:      and the schema is Fineract's, which HAS such objects.
ledger-invariants:   4. I-5. "A correction that mutates a leg instead of adding a reversing pair" is only
ledger-invariants:      decidable once a leg type and a correction path exist in the Go tree. Neither does. The
ledger-invariants:      mechanical half (a write to a committed row, a write to a balance) IS covered; the
ledger-invariants:      semantic half is not, and is not claimed.
ledger-invariants:   5. SEMANTICS OF DERIVATION. A function that CACHES a balance in a map keyed by account, then
ledger-invariants:      returns the cache, is caught only if the cache is named as a balance (class I3-PKG-STATE
ledger-invariants:      covers the package-level case). A struct-field cache inside a service object with a
ledger-invariants:      non-balance name is not.
ledger-invariants:   6. NON-GO CALLERS. Anything that reaches the database without going through this module.
ledger-invariants:   7. TWO MEASURED OVER-MATCHES, NAMED SO NOBODY REDISCOVERS THEM AS BUGS. (i) The DML-verb
ledger-invariants:      test fires on English prose: on nexus/ at the commit this guard was written, all three
ledger-invariants:      DML-classified literals are message text, not SQL — which is why every one is PRINTED
ledger-invariants:      with its position and why the NIL-COVERAGE notice keys on the narrower "names a table"
ledger-invariants:      count. (ii) The hold-name matcher fires on "...Holds" meaning "the property holds":
ledger-invariants:      nexus/internal/apps/ledger/slots_test.go:187 TestPlaceholderDisjointnessHolds is counted
ledger-invariants:      as a hold-named function. Neither over-match can produce a FINDING on its own — a finding
ledger-invariants:      additionally requires a protected table, a balance column, or a balance write — so both
ledger-invariants:      are noise in a count, never a false rejection.
ledger-invariants:   8. TESTS ARE INSPECTED, NOT EXEMPTED. A _test.go file that assigns a balance field as a
ledger-invariants:      FIXTURE is reported as a violation. That is fail-CLOSED by choice; if a legitimate
ledger-invariants:      fixture ever trips it, the fix is a constructor, not an exemption.
ledger-invariants: PASS — I-3 (balances are derived) and I-4 (append-only) hold over the
ledger-invariants:   Go tree AS FAR AS A SOURCE-LEVEL GUARD CAN SEE. Read the CANNOT-CATCH
ledger-invariants:   block and the NIL-COVERAGE lines before quoting this as coverage.
